the Hundred Years' War."}, {"sourceSlug": "richard-de-beauchamp-1st-earl-of-worcester", "sourceName": "Richard de Beauchamp, 1st Earl of Worcester", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "hundred-years-war", "targetName": "Hundred Years' War", "context": "Richard de Beauchamp was a key English commander during the Lancastrian phase of the Hundred Years' War, especially under Henry V."}, {"sourceSlug": "richard-de-beauchamp-1st-earl-of-worcester", "sourceName": "Richard de Beauchamp, 1st Earl of Worcester", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "elizabeth-de-beauchamp-baroness-bergavenny", "targetName": "Elizabeth de Beauchamp, Baroness Bergavenny", "context": "Richard de Beauchamp's death without a male heir meant his daughter, Elizabeth, inherited the Barony of Bergavenny."}], "places": [{"name": "Abergavenny, Wales", "role": "Baronial seat"}, {"name": "Rouen, France", "role": "Siege participation"}, {"name": "Meaux, France", "role": "Place of death"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Richard de Beauchamp, 1st Earl of Worcester", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "richard-de-beauchamp-1st-earl-of-worcester"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3934908", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Beauchamp,_1st_Earl_of_Worcester"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Richard de Beauchamp was a significant regional figure whose military contributions to King Henry V's campaigns in France were recognized with an earldom, though his early death limited his long-term impact. His career exemplifies the martial aristocracy of the early 15th century and the high cost of the Hundred Years' War on the English nobility.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-10-26T18:42:27Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40377324009", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Richard de Beauchamp, Earl of Worcester, KB was an English peer.", "newValue": "Richard de Beauchamp, 1st Earl of Worcester (c. 1397 – 18 March 1422), was a prominent English nobleman and military commander whose brief but impactful career unfolded during the tumultuous zenith of the Hundred Years' War. As a scion of one of England's most powerful baronial families, the Beauchamps of Warwick, his trajectory was intimately linked to the Lancastrian monarchy and its ambitious campaigns in France under King Henry V. His elevation to an earldom, a rare distinction, underscored his military contributions and the Crown's strategic need for loyal, capable leaders in a period of aggressive expansion and intense Anglo-French conflict, making him a representative figure of the martial aristocracy of early 15th-century England.

Beauchamp inherited the barony of Bergavenny (now Abergavenny) in 1411 upon the death of his father, William de Beauchamp, 1st Baron Bergavenny. His military service began early, leading to his knighting as a Knight of the Bath in 1413 at the coronation of King Henry V. He distinguished himself in the English campaigns in France, notably participating in the pivotal Siege of Rouen from July 1418 to January 1419, a strategic victory that secured Normandy for the English. His valor and loyalty were further recognized when he was created Earl of Worcester on 16 December 1421, a new creation of the earldom, making him one of the few individuals to hold such a title during Henry V's reign.

His promising career was tragically cut short when he died from a cannon shot during the protracted Siege of Meaux on 18 March 1422, fighting alongside King Henry V. The Earldom of Worcester became extinct upon his death, as he left only a daughter, Elizabeth de Beauchamp, who inherited the Barony of Bergavenny. His death, occurring just five months before King Henry V's own demise, symbolized the high cost of the Lancastrian ambitions in France and the personal sacrifices made by the English nobility in pursuit of a dual monarchy."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-26T18:42:27Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40377324009", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1394"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1394"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing Hundred Years' War provided opportunities for English noblemen like Beauchamp to distinguish themselves through military service and earn royal favor.", "His inheritance of the Barony of Bergavenny in 1411 positioned him as a significant landholder and peer, giving him the resources and status to pursue a military career.", "The strategic ambitions of King Henry V, particularly his aggressive campaigns in France, necessitated the loyalty and martial prowess of figures such as Richard de Beauchamp."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-26T18:42:27Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40377324009", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1422-03-18"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1422-03-18"}], "newValue": ["His death at the Siege of Meaux contributed to the attrition of English leadership during the Hundred Years' War, weakening the Lancastrian cause.", "The extinction of the Earldom of Worcester upon his death meant that the title did not pass to his heirs, highlighting the personal nature of some medieval peerages.", "His daughter, Elizabeth de Beauchamp, inherited the Barony of Bergavenny, ensuring the continuation of that ancient title within the family line."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-26T18:42:27Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40377324009", "field": "detailsJson.relationships", "oldValue": [{"sourceSlug": "richard-de-beauchamp-1st-earl-of-worcester", "sourceName": "Richard de Beauchamp, 1st Earl of Worcester", "verb": "COMMANDS", "targetSlug": "field-280", "targetName": "Military Leaders & Commanders", "context": "Richard de Beauchamp, 1st Earl of Worcester: Military command in military leaders & commanders"}, {"sourceSlug": "richard-de-beauchamp-1st-earl-of-worcester", "sourceName": "Richard de Beauchamp, 1st Earl of Worcester", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "medieval", "targetName": "Medieval Era", "context": "Richard de Beauchamp, 1st Earl of Worcester was active during the Medieval era"}], "newValue": [{"sourceSlug": "henry-v-of-england", "sourceName": "Henry V of England", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "richard-de-beauchamp-1st-earl-of-worcester", "targetName": "Richard de Beauchamp, 1st Earl of Worcester", "context": "King Henry V's military campaigns and trust in Beauchamp led to the latter's elevation and active service in France."}, {"sourceSlug": "richard-de-beauchamp-1st-earl-of-worcester", "sourceName": "Richard de Beauchamp, 1st Earl of Worcester", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "siege-of-rouen", "targetName": "Siege of Rouen", "context": "Richard de Beauchamp actively participated in the successful English Siege of Rouen from 1418 to 1419."}, {"sourceSlug": "richard-de-beauchamp-1st-earl-of-worcester", "sourceName": "Richard de Beauchamp, 1st Earl of Worcester", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "france", "targetName": "France", "context": "Richa